.TH CRABMON 1 "2026-11-22" "crabmon 0.19.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
crabmon \- terminal system monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crabmon
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crabmon
is a keyboard-driven terminal system monitor. It shows per-core CPU history,
memory and swap, a sortable and filterable process table with an optional tree
view and per-process disk IO, network throughput, per-mount disk usage,
throughput and saturation, temperature sensors, GPU utilisation and cgroup
limits. Given
.B [procs] fds
it also counts each process's open file descriptors against its own limit.
.PP
It also reports systemd services that have failed or are restarting in a loop,
kernel events such as OOM kills, and storage redundancy; evaluates alert rules
with hooks and built-in notifications, in the interface or headlessly; records
and replays sessions; exports Prometheus metrics; and watches other machines,
singly or as a fleet, over SSH. A unit's journal can be read, and the unit
restarted or reset, from the interface.
.PP
With
.B \-\-once
it prints a single snapshot as JSON, CSV or an aligned text table and exits,
which makes it usable from scripts and monitoring pipelines.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-refresh " " \fIMS\fR
Refresh interval in milliseconds (200\-10000). Values below 200 ms are clamped:
the underlying sampler needs at least that long between CPU samples for the
percentages to mean anything.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sort " " \fIKEY\fR
Initial sort column: \fBpid\fR, \fBname\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBmem\fR, \fBvirt\fR,
\fBdisk\fR, \fBtime\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBstate\fR, \fBthreads\fR, \fBnice\fR,
\fBfds\fR, \fBpss\fR, \fBgrowth\fR, \fBmajflt\fR, \fBswap\fR or \fBnet\fR.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-ascending
Sort ascending.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-descending
Sort descending. This is the default; the flag exists to override a config file
that asks for ascending.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-filter " " \fIQUERY\fR
Initial process filter. See \fBFILTER LANGUAGE\fR.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tree
Start in process-tree view.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-layout " " \fINAME\fR
Initial layout: \fBdashboard\fR, \fBprocesses\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBio\fR or
\fBfleet\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-theme " " \fINAME\fR
Colour theme: \fBdefault\fR, \fBmono\fR, \fBnord\fR, \fBsolarized\fR or
\fBgruvbox\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour. Equivalent to \fB\-\-theme mono\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mouse
Do not capture mouse events.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-once
Print one snapshot to standard output and exit instead of starting the TUI.
Always samples the host it runs on; combining it with \fB\-\-remote\fR or
\fB\-\-replay\fR is refused rather than quietly reporting the wrong machine.
.TP
.B \-\-format " " \fIFMT\fR
Output format for \fB\-\-once\fR and \fB\-\-watch\fR: \fBjson\fR (the default, via \fB[export] format\fR),
\fBcsv\fR or \fBtext\fR. \fBtext\fR is an aligned table laid out by the same
column planner the interface uses, i.e. the \fBtop \-b \-n1\fR shape.
\fB\-\-diff\fR writes its report as \fBjson\fR, as \fBcsv\fR \(em one row per
change, \fIsection,pid,name,metric,before,after,delta\fR, untruncated \(em or
otherwise as the text report.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-top " " \fIN\fR
Limit \fB\-\-once\fR and \fBP\fR snapshot output to the top \fIN\fR processes.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Use an alternate config file.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group " " \fIBY\fR
Aggregate the process table: \fBnone\fR, \fBservice\fR, \fBcontainer\fR or
\fBuser\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-columns " " \fILIST\fR
Process-table columns, comma separated, overriding \fB[procs] columns\fR \(em
e.g. \fBpid,user,cpu,mem,name\fR. Known columns: \fBmark\fR, \fBpid\fR,
\fBuser\fR, \fBstate\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBtrend\fR, \fBmem\fR, \fBvirt\fR,
\fBpss\fR, \fBswap\fR, \fBgrowth\fR, \fBmajflt\fR, \fBnet\fR, \fBthr\fR, \fBni\fR, \fBfd\fR,
\fBdisk\fR, \fBtime\fR, \fBports\fR, \fBname\fR. An unknown name is an error rather than a column quietly missing
from the table. \fBname\fR is always drawn last.
.TP
.B \-\-record " " \fIPATH\fR
Record every sample to a JSON Lines file, replacing any file already at that
path. See \fBRECORDINGS\fR. Works in every mode that samples on a loop: the
interface, \fB\-\-stream\fR, \fB\-\-serve\fR and \fB\-\-watch\fR.
\fB\-\-once\fR and \fB\-\-diff\fR refuse it, because a recording is a
sequence and they take one sample. Under \fB\-\-serve \-\-remote\fR each
host gets a file of its own, recorded when a new live frame is seen.
.TP
.B \-\-record\-rotate " " \fIDIR\fR
Keep a rotating recording in \fIDIR\fR, made if it does not exist; a path
that is not a directory stops crabmon at start. Segments of \fB[record]\fR
\fIrotate_frames\fR frames each, the oldest deleted once there are more than
\fIrotate_keep\fR of them. Unlike \fB\-\-record\fR this is meant to be left
on, so a replay of the last hour is always available without having predicted
the incident \(em and unlike the flight recorder it does not depend on an
alert rule having described what went wrong. Under \fB\-\-serve \-\-remote\fR
each host gets a directory of its own under \fIDIR\fR, so a fleet records a
timeline per machine rather than interleaving them into one. The directory is
the target reduced to something a filesystem will take, chosen over the whole
fleet so that two targets reducing to the same thing \(em \fBdeploy@db-2\fR
and \fBdeploy\-db\-2\fR both do \(em do not share a recording and truncate
each other's. A recording that cannot be written is reported once rather than
once a frame, and never stops the mode that is writing it. Available
headlessly on the same terms as \fB\-\-record\fR, which is what makes
.B crabmon \-\-serve :9100 \-\-alerts \-\-record\-rotate /var/log/crabmon
a complete black box in a unit file.
.TP
.B \-\-replay " " \fIPATH\fR
Drive the interface from a recording instead of sampling this host.
.TP
.B \-\-remote " " \fITARGET\fR[,\fITARGET\fR...]
Monitor \fITARGET\fR over SSH. crabmon must be installed on the far end and
SSH must work non-interactively; the refresh rate is bounded by the round trip.
A target written \fB@\fR\fIname\fR is every host of the \fB[[fleet]]\fR group
of that name; a host named more than once is watched once.
.PP
Several comma-separated targets open the \fBfleet\fR layout: one row per host,
sampled concurrently on a worker each, so one unreachable machine costs the
others nothing. \fBEnter\fR points the rest of the interface at the host under
the cursor; \fBW\fR returns to the table. A single target behaves exactly as it
always has, with no fleet table and no extra stop in the \fBTab\fR cycle.
.PP
Alert rules run on every host of a fleet, and the \fBALERT\fR column counts
those firing on each; hooks and notifications fire for any host, with
\fBCRABMON_HOST\fR naming it. \fB<\fR, \fB>\fR and \fBs\fR sort the
table; the \fIalerts\fR key puts hosts that are not answering first, then
the most rules firing.
.TP
.B \-\-remote\-command " " \fICMD\fR
Command to run on the remote host. Defaults to
\fBcrabmon \-\-stream \-\-refresh\fR \fIMS\fR, or, when \fB[remote] stream\fR
is false or the far end predates \fB\-\-stream\fR,
\fBcrabmon \-\-once \-\-refresh 200 \-\-format json \-\-top 0 \-\-filter=''\fR.
An explicit command is never second-guessed: one containing \fB\-\-stream\fR
as a word of its own is read as a stream, and anything else must print one
frame and exit \(em e.g. \fB/opt/crabmon/bin/crabmon \-\-stream\fR for a
crabmon that is not on the far end's \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-option " " \fIOPT\fR
Pass \fB\-o\fR \fIOPT\fR to \fBssh\fR for every \fB\-\-remote\fR connection,
e.g. \fBPort=2222\fR. Repeatable. ssh keeps the first value given for an
option, so these override \fB[remote] ssh_options\fR, which override
\fI~/.ssh/config\fR and crabmon's default \fBConnectTimeout=5\fR;
\fBBatchMode=yes\fR comes first and cannot be overridden, since a password
prompt would land in the middle of the interface.
Something that is not a \fIKEY\fB=\fIVALUE\fR option is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-serve " " \fIADDR\fR
Run headless and serve Prometheus metrics on \fIADDR\fR, e.g. \fB:9100\fR.
\fIADDR\fR may instead be a Unix socket \(em \fBunix:/run/crabmon.sock\fR,
or a bare path \(em in which case nothing listens on a port and the
filesystem does the access control; the socket is created mode \fB0660\fR.
A stale socket from a killed exporter is replaced, one still in use is not.
systemd units are exported as \fBcrabmon_systemd_units_failed\fR,
\fBcrabmon_systemd_units_crashlooping\fR and, for units that have failed or
restarted, \fBcrabmon_systemd_unit_restarts_total\fR and
\fBcrabmon_systemd_unit_state\fR; none of them where systemd was not read.
Routes: \fB/\fR, \fB/metrics\fR, \fB/healthz\fR. With \fB\-\-remote\fR it
exports a whole fleet instead, every series carrying \fBhost="..."\fR and each
host evaluating the \fB[[alert]]\fR rules on its own; flight dumps are then
named \fBcrabmon\-<host>\-<rule>\-<unix>.jsonl\fR. Combining it with
\fB\-\-replay\fR is an error: a recording has no \(lqnow\(rq to scrape.
.PP
A bare port binds to loopback; \fB:\fIPORT\fR binds to every interface. The
per-process series expose process names and PIDs, so the difference matters.
Command arguments are not exported as Prometheus labels. Connections
are handled concurrently and capped; past the cap callers get 503.
.TP
.B \-\-stream
Print one complete JSON snapshot per line, forever. Recording limits do not
trim transport frames: remote filters and alerts need every process. Command
arguments follow [export] redact_commands (true by default), so remote cmd:
filters see redacted arguments unless that setting is disabled remotely. This is what \fB\-\-remote\fR runs on the far end, and it is
usable on its own as a collector. Samples this host only.
.TP
.B \-\-alerts
With \fB\-\-serve\fR or \fB\-\-stream\fR, evaluate the \fB[[alert]]\fR rules
on every sample: run the hook commands, write flight recordings, and \(em under
\fB\-\-serve\fR \(em export each rule as \fBcrabmon_alert_active\fR,
\fBcrabmon_alert_value\fR, \fBcrabmon_alert_silenced\fR and
\fBcrabmon_alert_threshold\fR. \fB[[silence]]\fR windows apply. Without it those
rules run only in the full-screen interface, which means the flight recorder
depends on someone watching at the moment the incident happens. Notices go to
standard error, so \fB\-\-stream\fR's output stays one frame per line: a rule
is said when it starts firing, when its silence begins or ends, and when it
recovers, not on every sample in between.
.TP
.B \-\-diff " " \fIA\fR " " [\fIB\fR]
Compare two snapshots and print what changed: CPU, load, memory, swap,
per-filesystem usage, and the processes that started, exited and moved. Each
path may be a \fB\-\-once\fR export or a recording, in which case its last
frame is used. With one path, the recording's first frame is compared against
its last. \fB\-\-format json\fR emits the report as JSON.
.PP
Processes are matched on PID \fIand\fR start time, so a recycled PID reads as
one process exiting and another starting rather than as enormous growth.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Report what this machine will and will not let crabmon see \(em whether
\fB/dev/kmsg\fR and the RAPL counters are readable, whether pressure stall
accounting exists, whether another user's fd tables can be read \(em and
exit. crabmon never reports zero for something it could not read, which is
right and leaves "is that nothing, or something I am not allowed to see?" as
a question only this answers.
.IP
Exits \fB1\fR when something the config actually asks for cannot work here,
so it is usable as a check before a deploy and not only as something to read:
a missing GPU is not a failure, a \fBkind = "fd"\fR rule on a host whose fd
tables are unreadable is. \fB\-\-format json\fR emits the same report for
something other than a person.
.TP
.B \-\-watch " " \fIQUERY\fR
Sample headlessly until processes match \fIQUERY\fR (see \fBFILTER LANGUAGE\fR),
then print them and exit \fB1\fR. crabmon itself, whose command line carries
the query, and the programs it runs are not counted. Exits \fB0\fR if \fB\-\-watch\-timeout\fR
elapses first. A match is the non-zero exit, so \fB||\fR runs on a match and
\fB&&\fR on a clean run.
.TP
.B \-\-watch\-rule " " \fIEXPR\fR
As \fB\-\-watch\fR, but for a measurement rather than a process list:
\fIEXPR\fR is \fIkind\fR[:\fItarget\fR]\fIop\fR\fIvalue\fR, where
\fIkind\fR is any \fB[[alert]]\fR kind. \fBcpu>=90\fR, \fBpsi:io>20\fR,
\fBdisk:/var>=95\fR, \fBtemp>=80\fR, \fBfd>=90\fR, \fBnet>=1M\fR,
\fBproc:nginx<1\fR.
.PP
A \fBtrend\fR rule may name the series it follows in front of its query:
\fBtrend:postgres>200M\fR is resident memory, \fBtrend:io:postgres>10M\fR
the same processes' disk rate in bytes per second, and
\fBtrend:cpu:postgres>20\fR their CPU in percentage points. A \fBunit\fR rule
names its figure and then, optionally, the unit: \fBunit:failed>=1\fR,
\fBunit:restarts:api>=3\fR. The query keeps
its own colons, so \fBtrend:io:user:postgres>10M\fR is the IO rate of
everything that user runs.
.PP
\fBtrend\fR, \fBfillin\fR, \fBoomkill\fR and \fBanomaly\fR measure over a window, the
\fBover_secs\fR of an \fB[[alert]]\fR, given after the value as
\fB/\fR\fIDURATION\fR: \fBtrend:postgres>200M/10m\fR,
\fBfillin:/var<3h/30m\fR, \fBoomkill>=1/1h\fR. Without one they use five
minutes; any other kind refuses a window.
.PP
\fB>\fR and \fB>=\fR both mean at-or-over and \fB<\fR means strictly under,
which is how an \fB[[alert]]\fR compares; \fB<=\fR is refused rather than
quietly read as \fB<\fR. The operator is taken from the right, so a
\fBproc\fR query may contain one of its own: \fBproc:cpu>5<1\fR is "fewer
than one process over 5% CPU". A rule that cannot be parsed is an error, not a
watch that blocks until its timeout and then reports nothing happened; so is
a \fBdisk\fR or \fBfillin\fR rule naming a mount crabmon does not measure
(tmpfs and other memory filesystems are not), and a \fBunit\fR or
\fBkernel\fR rule whose source cannot be read here.
.TP
.B \-\-watch\-for " " \fISECS\fR
Require the match to hold this many consecutive seconds. A condition that
flickers in and out never accumulates credit across the gaps. Default 0.
.TP
.B \-\-watch\-timeout " " \fISECS\fR
Give up after this long. 0, the default, waits forever.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B q, Ctrl\-C
Quit.
.TP
.B Up/Down, k/j, PgUp/PgDn, Home/End
Move the selection.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the process detail pane, or \(em in the grouped view \(em open the selected
group, filtering the process table down to the processes inside it.
.TP
.B W
Show the fleet table, or go back to the dashboard from it. Only meaningful
when crabmon was started with several \fB\-\-remote\fR targets.
.TP
.B /
Filter processes. \fBEsc\fR clears the filter, \fBCtrl\-U\fR the line and \fBCtrl\-W\fR its last word.
.TP
.B c, m, p, n, d
Sort by CPU, memory, PID, name or disk IO. Pressing the active column's key
again reverses the order.
.TP
.BR < ", " > " (or " , " and " . )
Move to the previous or next sort column. In the fleet table these, and
\fBs\fR, sort the hosts instead.
.TP
.B s
Reverse the sort order.
.TP
.B T
Toggle the process tree.
.TP
.B H
Show or hide individual threads.
.TP
.B G
Cycle grouping: none, systemd unit, container, user. The aggregate table has its
own cursor; process actions are refused from it, since it shows no process rows.
.TP
.B Space
Tag the selected process. Signals, renice, affinity and IO priority then apply to every
tagged process instead of just the cursor.
.TP
.B U
Untag everything.
.TP
.B f
Pin the selected process. Pinned rows are held at the top of the table whatever
the sort, and stay visible through a filter that would otherwise hide them, so
one process can be watched while the rest churns.
.TP
.B F
Unpin everything.
.TP
.B z
Pause and resume sampling.
.TP
.BR [ ", " ]
Step a recording back or forward one frame;
.BR { " and " }
step ten.
.TP
.BR ( ", " )
Jump to the previous or next marker in a recording: a frame where an
\fB[[alert]]\fR rule fires, run over the recording on its own clock, or where
a kernel event first appears. The bar in the status line shows them.
.TP
.B :
Go to a frame in a recording: \fB#120\fR is a frame number, \fB5m\fR that
long from the start, \fB+30s\fR and \fB\-2m\fR relative to the current frame.
.TP
.B 1-9
Apply the correspondingly numbered saved filter;
.B 0
clears the filter.
.TP
.B y
Copy the selected process's command line to the system clipboard using OSC 52,
which works through SSH and tmux.
.TP
.B L
Show the local log of actions crabmon has taken.
.TP
.BR j ", " k ", " PgUp ", " PgDn " (in a popup)"
Scroll it. The key list, the alert log and the action log are all longer than a
short terminal can show; the title says which part is on screen.
.TP
.B Tab, Shift\-Tab
Cycle layouts.
.TP
.B v
Include or exclude virtual interfaces (loopback, bridges, veth, VPN tunnels)
from the aggregate network rate.
.TP
.B g
Group or expand temperature sensors.
.TP
.B C
Cycle the compact layout: auto, on, off.
.TP
.BR b ", " B
Screen brighter or dimmer by a tenth of its range, never to black. Needs write
access to \fI/sys/class/backlight\fR (root, the \fBvideo\fR group or a udev
rule), and only acts on the machine crabmon is running on.
.TP
.BR + ", " \- " (or " = " and " _ )
Refresh faster or slower.
.TP
.B t
Signal menu for the selected process (Unix). A confirmation prompt follows.
.TP
.B r
Change the selected process's nice value (Unix).
.TP
.B A
Set the selected process's CPU affinity (Linux).
.TP
.B I
Set the selected process's IO scheduling priority (Linux), the way
.BR ionice (1)
spells it: a class of
.BR none ,
.BR rt ,
.B be
or
.BR idle ,
and a level of 0\(en7 for the two classes that have one.
.TP
.B o ", " O
Fold or unfold the subtree under the cursor in the tree view; a folded row is
marked with the number of rows it hides, e.g. \fB+40 chrome\fR.
.B O
unfolds everything. Folds are kept by PID, so they survive a refresh and go
when the process does.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-R
Reload the config file: thresholds, colours, panels, columns and
\fB[[alert]]\fR rules are re-read and applied without restarting. Session
state \(em the filter, tags, pins, selection, layout and sort \(em is kept.
A file that does not parse changes nothing.
.TP
.B P
Write a snapshot to the export directory.
.TP
.B S
Silence alert rules for a while: \fB1h\fR for every rule, \fB30m\fR
\fIrule\fR for one, \fBoff\fR [\fIrule\fR] to lift silences set this way
(\fB[[silence]]\fR windows stay). Also in the \fB!\fR popup. A silenced rule
is still evaluated and shown, but its hooks and notifications are held; a
rule still firing when the silence ends pages then, and one that fired and
recovered inside it says nothing.
.TP
.B u
Open a systemd unit: the selected group when grouping by service, else the
selected process's. Shows what systemd says, the unit's recent journal
(carried in the snapshot for units in trouble, read on the spot for any local
unit that did not bring one), and offers \fBr\fR to restart it and \fBx\fR to
\fBreset\-failed\fR it, each confirmed with \fBy\fR, logged, and run with
\fBsystemctl \-\-no\-ask\-password\fR; \fBl\fR reloads the journal. Read-only
for a unit on another machine.
.TP
.B E
The events timeline, newest first: rules firing and clearing, kernel events,
units failing, restarting and recovering, storage arrays and SMART disks
changing state, fleet hosts that stop or start answering, and actions taken
from the interface. Times are UTC; a unit or disk already in trouble at start
is marked \fB(at start)\fR. Worked out from the samples, so a replay rebuilds
it; the last 500 are kept.
.TP
.B !
Show the alert rules and their state, every transition, and the last fifty
notes from running hooks and notifications \(em a post that failed, a hook
killed after its timeout, a flight recording written.
.TP
.BR ? " (or " F1 )
Show all key bindings.
.SH FILTER LANGUAGE
Terms are separated by whitespace and combined with AND. A leading \fB!\fR
negates a term. A standalone \fB|\fR is OR, and binds looser than the implicit
AND, so \fBa b | c\fR means \fB(a AND b) OR c\fR. Only a \fB|\fR surrounded
by whitespace is an operator, so a regular expression containing one is left
alone. A \fIword\fB:\fIvalue\fR term whose word is not a field below is an
error naming the fields; a name containing a colon, such as
\fBkworker/u8:2\fR, is still a name.
.PP
.nf
firefox            name contains "firefox" (also name:firefox)
!kworker           name does not contain "kworker"
nginx | apache     either one
user:vlad          user name contains "vlad"
pid:1234           exact PID
ppid:1             exact parent PID
state:R            process state letter
service:sshd       systemd unit; service:\- means "no unit"
container:abc123   container id or name; container:\- means "not in one"
cmd:\-\-headless     substring of the full command line
re:^chrom(e|ium)$  regular expression over the process name
cpu>5              CPU percentage comparison (> >= < <= =)
mem>100M           resident memory comparison, with K/M/G/T suffixes
virt>2G            virtual size, same suffixes
io>1M              disk IO rate comparison
thr>50             thread count
nice<0             scheduling priority
time>2h            run time, in s/m/h/d; a bare number is seconds
fd>1000            open file descriptors (needs [procs] fds)
fd%>90             ...as a percentage of the process's own limit
pss>500M           proportional set size (needs [procs] pss)
uss>100M           unique set size, same suffixes
.fi
.PP
A process that does not report a value never satisfies a comparison on it: a
thread has no thread count, and another user's process reports no descriptors.
Reading either as zero would sweep every one of them into \fBthr<2\fR and
\fBfd<100\fR.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/crabmon/config.toml
(usually
.IR ~/.config/crabmon/config.toml ).
The file is written when the refresh interval changes and on exit. A fleet
session keeps the layout the file had, and \fBfleet\fR itself is never
saved, since it means nothing without \fB\-\-remote\fR hosts. The active
filter is session state and is not written back, so a query typed at
.B /
does not silently narrow later runs. Missing or malformed files fall back to
defaults; out-of-range values are clamped rather than rejected \(em except
\fB[[notify]]\fR and \fB[[silence]]\fR entries that cannot work, which stop
crabmon at start, and an \fB[[alert]]\fR whose \fBhysteresis\fR is negative or
not finite, which makes the whole file invalid so the defaults are used, with
a warning.
.SS Top-level keys
.TP
.B refresh_ms
Refresh interval in milliseconds, clamped to 200\-10000. Default 800.
.TP
.B sort_by
Sort column (pid, name, cpu, mem, virt, disk, time, user, state, threads, nice, fds, pss, growth, majflt, swap, net). Default cpu.
.TP
.B sort_desc
Sort descending. Default true.
.TP
.B filter
Initial filter query. Default empty.
.TP
.B tree
Start in process-tree view. Default false.
.TP
.B show_threads
List individual threads alongside processes. Default false: on Linux the
sampler reports every task, which is several times more rows than processes.
.TP
.B group_by
Aggregate the process table: \fBnone\fR, \fBservice\fR, \fBcontainer\fR or
\fBuser\fR. Default \fBnone\fR.
.TP
.B layout
\fBdashboard\fR, \fBprocesses\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBio\fR or \fBfleet\fR.
Default \fBdashboard\fR.
.TP
.B compact
\fBauto\fR, \fBon\fR or \fBoff\fR: the small-screen dashboard, a few lines of
vitals over a full-width process table. \fBauto\fR (the default) uses it on a
terminal smaller than 100 by 30; \fBC\fR cycles it.
.TP
.B theme
\fBdefault\fR, \fBmono\fR, \fBnord\fR, \fBsolarized\fR or \fBgruvbox\fR.
.TP
.B group_sensors
Collapse temperature sensors to one row per driver. Default true.
.TP
.B show_virtual_ifaces
Count loopback, bridges, veth pairs and tunnels in the aggregate network rate.
Default false, so VPN traffic is not counted twice.
.TP
.B virtual_iface_prefixes
Interface-name prefixes treated as virtual. Defaults to \fBlo\fR, \fBdocker\fR,
\fBbr\-\fR, \fBveth\fR, \fBvirbr\fR, \fBtun\fR, \fBtap\fR, \fBwg\fR,
\fBvmnet\fR, \fBzt\fR, \fBcni\fR, \fBflannel\fR, \fBkube\fR and \fButun\fR.
.TP
.B history_len
Samples kept per chart, clamped to 16\-4096. Default 240.
.TP
.B panel_order
Draw order of the right-hand column. Default
.BR net ", " psi ", " kernel ", " sensors ", " disks ", " power ", " gpu ", " cgroup .
.SS [thresholds]
.B warn
and
.B crit
are the 0..1 ratios at which gauges turn yellow and red (0.7 and 0.9).
.B temp_warn
and
.B temp_crit
are the same, as a fraction of each sensor's critical temperature (0.75 and
0.9).
.SS [panels]
.BR header ", " cpu ", " mem ", " procs ", " net ", " sensors ", " disks ", "
.BR gpu ", " cgroup ", " psi ", " kernel " and " power
each switch a panel off when set to false. All default to true. The
.BR psi ", " power ", " gpu " and " cgroup
panels are additionally hidden when the machine has nothing to report.
.SS [gpu]
.B enabled
draws the GPU panel.
.B nvidia_smi
additionally runs \fBnvidia\-smi\fR once per refresh, on a thread of its own so a
slow driver delays only the GPU figures (one sample behind, and dropped after
15 seconds without an answer); off by default because it costs tens of
milliseconds.
.PP
On ARM boards the GPU is a platform device that the DRM scan cannot read, so it
is found separately: Mali and other SoC GPUs through devfreq (clock, ceiling, a
load figure where the driver has one, the GPU's own thermal zone where the
board has one), and a Raspberry Pi's VideoCore through \fBvcgencmd\fR (its
clock, and the memory reserved for it). A VideoCore has no load figure without
debugfs, so its row shows the clock rather than a zero. \fB\-\-doctor\fR says
when \fBvcgencmd\fR is missing.
.SS [export]
.B dir
is where \fBP\fR writes snapshots (empty means the working directory),
.B format
is \fBjson\fR, \fBcsv\fR or \fBtext\fR, and
.B top_n
limits the process list (0 keeps every process).
.B redact_commands
hides common credential arguments in exports and streams (default true).
.SS [record]
.B top_n
processes are kept per frame, busiest first, and real processes are preferred
over threads (default 100; 0 keeps everything, at roughly 1.5 MB a frame).
.B max_cmd_len
truncates command lines (default 200) and
.B omit_paths
drops them, along with executable paths and working directories, entirely.
.B redact_commands
hides common credential arguments before truncation (default true), including
plain, rotating and flight recordings and incident summaries.
.PP
.B flight
turns on the flight recorder: that many frames of context are held in memory at
all times and written out, together with the next
.B flight_after
frames, whenever an alert fires \(em so the recording of an incident exists
because the incident happened rather than because someone predicted it.
Default 0, which switches it off.
.B flight_dir
is where dumps are written (empty means the working directory); each is named
\fBcrabmon\-<rule>\-<unix>.jsonl\fR and replays like any other recording. At
the default 800 ms refresh, \fBflight = 750\fR is ten minutes and roughly
40 MB of live memory. A rule that flaps extends the dump in progress rather
than opening a file per refresh. Beside each dump crabmon writes
\fBcrabmon\-<rule>\-<unix>.md\fR: what fired and by how much, the machine at
that moment, what the kernel had said, the busiest processes, and what changed
over the frames before it \(em a JSONL recording is evidence nobody can read
without crabmon in front of it.
.PP
.B rotate_frames
and
.B rotate_keep
bound a rotating recording (\fB\-\-record\-rotate\fR): frames per segment
file, and segments kept including the one being written. 0 (the default)
leaves rotation off unless \fB\-\-record\-rotate\fR is given, which then uses
1800; a non-zero value turns rotation on by itself. Segments go to
\fBrotate_dir\fR, falling back to \fBflight_dir\fR and then the working
directory. At the default 800 ms refresh,
\fBrotate_frames = 1800\fR with \fBrotate_keep = 4\fR is about ninety
minutes on disk. Under \fB\-\-serve\fR a frame is recorded per scrape
rather than per \fIrefresh_ms\fR, because a scrape is when a sample is
actually taken, so size \fIrotate_frames\fR against the scrape interval
there: the same 1800 is about seven hours at a fifteen-second scrape.
.B rotate_dir
is where segments go when the flag names no directory.
.SS [procs]
.B columns
fixes the column order instead of fitting the terminal width. Names are
\fBmark\fR, \fBpid\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBstate\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBtrend\fR,
\fBmem\fR, \fBpss\fR, \fBswap\fR, \fBgrowth\fR, \fBmajflt\fR, \fBnet\fR, \fBvirt\fR, \fBthr\fR, \fBni\fR,
\fBfd\fR, \fBdisk\fR, \fBtime\fR, \fBports\fR and \fBname\fR; COMMAND is always drawn last and
unknown names are ignored. Empty (the default) fits as many as will go.
.B pss
reads \fB/proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup\fR for each process, which adds the
\fBPSS\fR column and the \fBpss>\fR and \fBuss>\fR filters. RSS counts a
page shared between eight processes eight times, so a tree's RSS can exceed
the machine's memory; PSS divides each shared page among its holders and adds
up to something real, and USS is what killing the process would actually free.
It also supplies each process's swapped memory, the \fBSWAP\fR column and
\fB\-s swap\fR.
Off by default: one more read per process per sample. Threads are skipped,
since every thread of a process shares its address space and would report the
process's own figures.
.TP
.B net
measures each process's TCP traffic from \fBss\fR, which adds the \fBNET/s\fR
column and \fB\-s net\fR. TCP only (UDP and QUIC read as quiet), only sockets
open during the interval are counted, and other users' processes need root.
Off by default: one \fBss\fR run per sample.
.TP
.B ports
looks up listening ports for the rows on screen \(em one fd table per visible row,
so it is off by default.
.B pin_marker
draws the leading marker column (\fB\(bu\fR tagged, pinned).
.SS [units]
.B enabled
reads systemd's services with \fBsystemctl show\fR every five seconds, on a
thread of its own (default true; Linux only).
.B crash_loop
automatic restarts within
.B window_secs
seconds make a unit crash-looping (defaults 3 and 600). A unit with
\fBActiveState=failed\fR is failed. Either is named in the header and the
fleet table's STATUS column; grouping by service gives such units a row, with
a \fBUNIT\fR column, even when nothing of theirs is running. Restarts that
happened before crabmon started are not counted toward a loop.
.PP
.B redact_logs
(default true) hides credential values in the journal lines units in trouble
carry, on the unit's own machine before they reach a snapshot: the value
after a credential-looking \fIkey\fB=\fR, \fIkey\fB:\fR or quoted JSON
key, the credential after an \fBAuthorization:\fR scheme, bearer tokens,
and URL user and password. The words around them are kept, since a journal
line is the reason a unit is down.
.SS [smart]
.B enabled
(default false) reads disk health from SMART through \fBsmartctl\fR
(smartmontools 7.0 or later), every
.B interval_secs
seconds (default 600, at least 60) on a thread of its own, with
\fB\-n standby\fR so a disk that has spun down is not woken and keeps its last
reading.
.B command
is how to run it, split on whitespace (default \fBsmartctl\fR); reading SMART
needs root, so \fBsudo \-n smartctl\fR with a sudoers rule for that binary is
the unprivileged way.
.B devices
lists device paths; empty (the default) reads whatever \fBsmartctl \-\-scan\fR
finds. Each disk becomes a storage row of kind \fBsmart\fR: failed when its
self-assessment fails or a pre-fail attribute is below threshold now, degraded
with remapped, pending or uncorrectable sectors, NVMe media errors, a critical
warning, spare capacity below its threshold or endurance used up. So it fires
\fBstorage\fR rules and is exported as \fBcrabmon_storage_degraded\fR, with
per-disk \fBcrabmon_smart_temperature_celsius\fR, \fBcrabmon_smart_power_on_hours\fR,
\fBcrabmon_smart_wear_percent\fR, \fBcrabmon_smart_reallocated_sectors\fR,
\fBcrabmon_smart_pending_sectors\fR, \fBcrabmon_smart_uncorrectable_sectors\fR,
\fBcrabmon_smart_media_errors\fR and \fBcrabmon_smart_passed\fR. When no
disk can be read the reason is reported by \fB\-\-doctor\fR, which fails while
\fB[smart]\fR is on, and as \fBsmart_unreadable\fR in JSON.
.SS [remote]
.B stream
holds one SSH session open and reads a stream of frames instead of starting a
process per sample, falling back automatically when the far end predates
\fB\-\-stream\fR. Default true.
.PP
.B ssh_options
is a list of \fBssh_config\fR options passed as \fB\-o\fR on every
connection. \fB\-\-ssh\-option\fR overrides them, and they override
\fI~/.ssh/config\fR and the default \fBConnectTimeout=5\fR, but not
\fBBatchMode=yes\fR. Default empty.
.SS [serve]
.B top_procs
per-process series are exported by \fB\-\-serve\fR, busiest first (default
20; 0 disables them).
.PP
.B token
requires every scrape of \fB/metrics\fR to present
\fBAuthorization: Bearer <token>\fR; an empty token (the default) leaves the
exporter open. The environment variable \fBCRABMON_SERVE_TOKEN\fR overrides
it, which keeps the secret out of a config file. A scrape exposes host metrics
and process names and PIDs; it does not include command arguments. \fB/healthz\fR is
exempt, and the comparison is constant-time.
.SS [audit]
.B enabled
records every signal, renice, affinity and IO-priority change and every unit
restart or \fBreset\-failed\fR; \fBpath\fR overrides the
default location under the platform state directory.
.B max_bytes
bounds it: at that size the log is moved to \fIpath\fB.1\fR, replacing the
previous one, and started again (default 1 MiB; 0 never rotates). The
\fBL\fR viewer reads across both.
.SS [[filter_preset]]
Each table binds a
.B name
and a
.B query
to the next number key, in order. \fB$USER\fR is expanded. At most nine are
used, since there are only nine number keys. Declaring any preset replaces the
built-in five \(em \fBbusy\fR (\fBcpu>5\fR), \fBhungry\fR (\fBmem>500M\fR),
\fBmine\fR (\fBuser:$USER\fR), \fBio\fR (\fBio>100K\fR) and \fBstuck\fR
(\fBstate:D\fR) \(em rather than adding to them.
.SS [colors]
Overrides individual theme colours by name:
.BR ok ", " warn ", " crit ", " accent ", " border ", " title ", " text ", "
.BR dim ", " sel_fg ", " sel_bg .
Values may be colour names (\fBred\fR), hex (\fB#ff8800\fR) or 256-colour
indexes (\fB33\fR). Unknown keys and unparseable values are ignored.
.SS [[fleet]]
Each table names a host list for \fB\-\-remote @\fR\fIname\fR:
.B name
and
.BR hosts ,
a list of SSH targets.
.SS [[silence]]
Each table is a maintenance window in which rules page nobody: they are
still evaluated, shown and recorded, but hooks and notifications are held,
and sent when the window closes if the rule is still firing.
.B comment
says what it is for;
.B rules
and
.B hosts
(fleet target or hostname) limit it, empty meaning all; and it is either
.BR daily ,
\fBHH:MM\-HH:MM\fR every day, or
.B start
and
.BR end ,
like \fB2026\-11\-20T02:00Z\fR \(em all in UTC. A malformed window
stops crabmon at start; a reload keeps the old windows instead.
.SS [[notify]]
Each table posts every alert transition \(em of every rule, with or without a
\fBcommand\fR, outside a silence \(em to one destination.
.B kind
is \fBwebhook\fR (JSON carrying the hook variables, lower-cased without the
prefix), \fBslack\fR or \fBdiscord\fR (a chat message), or \fBntfy\fR (the
message, with title, priority and tag headers).
.B url
is where to post, and must be \fBhttp://\fR or \fBhttps://\fR;
.B token
is sent as \fBAuthorization: Bearer\fR when set;
.B rules
limits it to rules by name (empty: every rule); and
.B cleared
posts recoveries too (default true).
.B name
labels it in messages. The post is made by \fBcurl\fR, which must be
installed, with the URL, token and body on its standard input rather than its
command line; a failed post is reported, and one taking over 15 seconds is
abandoned. A target crabmon cannot use stops it at start.
.SS [[alert]]
Each table defines one rule:
.IR name ", " kind ", " threshold ", " hysteresis ", " for_secs ", " over_secs ", " below ", " target ", "
.IR query ", " command " and " command_clear .
.I kind
is one of
.IR cpu ", " mem ", " swap ", " load ", " disk ", " temp ", " gpu ", " psi ", "
.IR io ", " net ", " fd ", " proc ", " trend ", " fillin ", " cgroup ", "
.IR kernel ", " cputime ", " vm ", " throttle ", " oomkill ", " tcp ", " storage ", "
.IR unit ", " battery ", " soc " or " anomaly .
.I battery
reads the lowest charge by default (use \fBbelow\fR), or \fBhealth\fR,
\fBremaining\fR (seconds, for batteries that are running down),
\fBdraw\fR, \fBcycles\fR or \fBtemp\fR.
.I soc
reads a Raspberry Pi's firmware throttle word: \fBthrottled\fR counts the
conditions in effect now (undervoltage, frequency cap, throttling, soft
temperature limit), \fBundervoltage\fR is 1 while the supply sags,
\fBsinceboot\fR counts any seen since boot and \fBclock\fR is the CPU clock
as a percentage of its ceiling.
.I anomaly
is how many standard deviations the series named by \fItarget\fR (\fBcpu\fR,
\fBmem\fR, \fBswap\fR, \fBload\fR, \fBio\fR, \fBnet\fR or \fBtemp\fR) is from
its own recent past over \fIover_secs\fR; a drop is \fBbelow\fR with a
negative threshold. It says nothing until the window is half full.
.I unit
measures systemd services: \fItarget\fR is \fBcrashloop\fR (units restarting in
a loop, the default), \fBfailed\fR, or \fBrestarts\fR (the most automatic
restarts of any unit within \fB[units] window_secs\fR), and \fIquery\fR names
a unit, \fBapi\fR or \fBapi.service\fR; empty means all of them. It measures
nothing where systemd cannot be read.
.I storage
counts md arrays, ZFS pools, btrfs filesystems and \fB[smart]\fR disks that are
degraded, rebuilding or failed;
.I target
names one (\fBmd0\fR, a pool, a btrfs label, a disk such as \fBsdb\fR) and an
empty target counts all of them. The message names them and their reasons.
.I throttle
is the share of CPU-quota periods a container or unit spent throttled, in
percent;
.I target
names one by id, unit or container name, and an empty target takes the
worst.
.I oomkill
counts OOM kills within \fIover_secs\fR (default 300): inside the named
container or unit, or, with an empty target, on the whole machine from
\fI/proc/vmstat\fR, which needs no root. A named unit whose cgroup is
already gone \(em it died between samples and systemd removed it \(em is
counted from the kernel's kill line naming the cgroup, or from the unit
being failed with \fBResult=oom-kill\fR.
.I tcp
reads the figure its target names: \fBretrans\fR (percent of segments
sent, the default), \fBlistendrops\fR, \fBresets\fR or \fBfailed\fR
(per second), or \fBclosewait\fR, \fBtimewait\fR or \fBestab\fR
(counts).
.I cputime
is the share of CPU time spent in the mode
.I target
names \(em \fBuser\fR, \fBnice\fR, \fBsystem\fR, \fBiowait\fR,
\fBirq\fR, \fBsoftirq\fR, \fBsteal\fR or \fBidle\fR \(em in percent,
from \fI/proc/stat\fR; an empty target is everything but idle.
.I vm
is the paging rate from \fI/proc/vmstat\fR: \fBswapin\fR, \fBswapout\fR
or \fBswap\fR (both, the default) in bytes per second, or \fBmajfault\fR
in major faults per second. Unlike \fIswap\fR, which is how much is held,
it fires on a machine that is thrashing.
.I cgroup
is a container's or unit's memory against its own cgroup limit, which the
host's gauges cannot see;
.I target
names one, and an empty target watches whichever is closest to its limit.
.PP
.I fillin
is seconds until a filesystem is full at the rate it is filling, so it is
written with
.I below
\(em less time is the alarming direction. An empty
.I target
watches whichever mount fills first. The same figure is drawn in the disks
panel for any mount that will fill within a week.
.PP
.I trend
measures growth rather than level: \fIover_secs\fR is the window (default
300) and a \fIquery\fR selects the processes measured \(em the worst grower
wins \(em while an empty query measures the machine's \fImem\fR or
\fIswap\fR in bytes. With a query, \fItarget\fR names which of those
processes' series to follow: \fBrss\fR (the default), \fBcpu\fR or
\fBio\fR. \fIthreshold\fR is read in that series' own unit \(em bytes for
\fBrss\fR, bytes per second for \fBio\fR, percentage points for \fBcpu\fR
\(em and a \fItarget\fR naming no series measures nothing rather than
quietly reporting \fBrss\fR under another name.
A rule fires only once its condition has held for \fIfor_secs\fR seconds, and
its \fIcommand\fR runs once per activation. \fIcommand_clear\fR runs once when
it recovers \(em both only outside a silence (see \fB[[silence]]\fR) \(em and
the alert popup (\fB!\fR) logs every transition,
so an alert that fired at 03:12 and cleared at 03:14 says both.
.PP
A hook runs as \fBsh \-c\fR \fIcommand\fR with the context in its
environment, so one script can serve every rule:
.BR CRABMON_RULE ,
.BR CRABMON_KIND ,
.B CRABMON_STATE
(\fBfiring\fR or \fBcleared\fR),
.BR CRABMON_VALUE ,
.BR CRABMON_THRESHOLD ,
.BR CRABMON_MESSAGE ,
.B CRABMON_HOST
(the fleet target, or this machine's name),
.BR CRABMON_AT ,
and
.B CRABMON_DUMP
and
.B CRABMON_SUMMARY
for the flight recording a firing opened and the readable summary beside it,
and
.B CRABMON_DETAIL
\(em for a \fBunit\fR rule, the failing unit's last journal lines, which
\fB[[notify]]\fR posts under the message.
Every one is always set, empty where it does not apply, so a hook written with
\fBset \-u\fR need not guess which exist. Output goes nowhere and the hook is
not waited on: one that blocks the sampler is worse than no hook at all.
A hook still running after 60 seconds is killed together with everything it
started, and the status line says so; one that needs longer must detach.
.PP
.I threshold
is a percentage for \fIcpu\fR, \fImem\fR, \fIswap\fR, \fIdisk\fR,
\fIgpu\fR, \fIpsi\fR, \fIio\fR and \fIfd\fR, degrees for \fItemp\fR,
bytes per second for \fInet\fR, a plain count for \fIproc\fR and an absolute
figure for \fIload\fR.
.I below
fires when the measurement is strictly under the threshold instead of at or
over it, which is the only way to say "fewer than one nginx". The comparison is
strict where the default is inclusive, so \fBthreshold = 1\fR on a \fIproc\fR
rule means "none running" rather than "one or fewer".
.PP
.I target
restricts disk and io rules to a mount point, temperature rules to a sensor
prefix, GPU rules to a device name, net rules to an interface and fd rules to a
process name. For \fIpsi\fR it names the resource \(em \fBcpu\fR,
\fBmem\fR or \fBio\fR, optionally suffixed \fB.full\fR \(em and an empty
target is the worst \fBsome\fR reading of the three. For a \fItrend\fR rule
with a \fIquery\fR it names the series to follow: \fBrss\fR, \fBcpu\fR or
\fBio\fR.
.I query
is the filter query a \fIproc\fR rule counts matches of; a query that does not
parse leaves the rule inert rather than counting zero.
.PP
.I io
is device utilisation, not capacity: the figure that says a disk is the
bottleneck when it is serving small random reads at a few MB/s and both its
throughput and its free space look fine.
.I fd
measures each process against its own descriptor limit, which needs
\fB[procs] fds\fR.
.SH RECORDINGS
.B \-\-record
writes one JSON object per sample, so a recording is greppable with
.BR jq (1)
and replayable with
.BR "crabmon \-\-replay" .
Frames are trimmed by \fB[record]\fR \(em by default the busiest 100 processes
with command lines truncated to 200 characters, roughly 55 KiB per frame, or
about 240 MiB for an hour at the default interval.
.PP
While replaying, sampling is paused: \fB[\fR and \fB]\fR step one frame,
\fB{\fR and \fB}\fR step ten, and \fBz\fR plays on. Every panel renders
from the recording exactly as it would from the live host.
.PP
The status line draws the recording as a bar with the current frame and a
mark wherever something happened: \fB!\fR where an \fB[[alert]]\fR rule
fires, run over the recording on its own clock, and \fBk\fR where a kernel
event first appears. \fB(\fR and \fB)\fR jump between the marks; \fB:\fR
goes to \fB#\fR\fIframe\fR, a time from the start such as \fB5m\fR, or a
relative one such as \fB+30s\fR. The marks and frame times come from one
pass over the file on a background thread.
.SS Flight recorder
.B \-\-record
only helps if it was started before the incident, which is the one thing nobody
does. With
.B [record] flight
set, crabmon holds that many recent frames in memory at all times and writes
them out \(em plus
.B flight_after
further frames \(em whenever an alert fires. The result is an ordinary
recording named \fBcrabmon\-<rule>\-<unix>.jsonl\fR, readable by
.B \-\-replay
and
.BR \-\-diff .
Frames are trimmed by the same \fB[record]\fR limits on the way into the ring,
so the window costs what a recording of it would.
.SH PLATFORM NOTES
Sending signals and renice are Unix-only. CPU affinity, GPU statistics
(\fI/sys/class/drm\fR), cgroup limits, process grouping, open-socket lookup,
the file-descriptor count (\fI/proc/<pid>/fd\fR and \fI/proc/<pid>/limits\fR)
and pressure stall information are Linux-only; those panels and columns are
simply absent elsewhere. Pressure needs Linux 4.20+ with \fBCONFIG_PSI=y\fR.
.PP
Per-disk IO, utilisation and request latency come from \fI/proc/diskstats\fR on
Linux and from
.BR iostat (8)
on FreeBSD, where they are since-boot averages rather than the interval rates
the Linux path derives. The FreeBSD path is compile-checked and unit-tested
against captured output but has not been exercised on real hardware.
.PP
Battery and mains state need no privileges. CPU package power comes from RAPL,
whose counters are root-only on most kernels since CVE-2020-8694; the figure is
omitted rather than guessed when they cannot be read. Per-process disk IO
requires permission to read \fI/proc/<pid>/io\fR, so figures for other users'
processes read as zero unless crabmon runs as root; their fd tables are
unreadable for the same reason, and report as unknown rather than zero.
.PP
Alert rules \(em and so the flight recorder \(em are evaluated in the
interface and, with \fB\-\-alerts\fR, under \fB\-\-serve\fR and
\fB\-\-stream\fR; \fB\-\-watch\fR is a rule by construction.
.BR \-\-once " and " \-\-diff
take a single sample and never fire one.
.SH SAMPLE FRESHNESS, HYSTERESIS AND REDACTION
Remote exporters emit crabmon_sample_age_seconds (seconds since receipt of a
new frame) and crabmon_sample_stale (1 when connecting, failed or stale).
Age is omitted until the first sample. Stale timeout is the larger of five
seconds and three refresh intervals. These use the collector's monotonic clock.
.PP
An alert's hysteresis defaults to 0 and must be finite and nonnegative.
For an above rule, recovery requires a
value strictly below threshold minus hysteresis. For a below rule, recovery
requires a value at or above threshold plus hysteresis. The margin applies
only after firing; hold times still apply to the original threshold.
.PP
[export] and [record] each accept redact_commands = true (the default).
Common credential arguments, bearer tokens and URL user/password pairs are
hidden before truncation. Set false to retain raw command lines. This applies
to exported snapshots, streams, plain/rotating recordings and flight dumps.
Local live process filters and display retain the original data. Remote views
receive the remote host's exported, possibly redacted command. Unquoted
credential values conservatively hide the rest of the command, since joined
argv cannot preserve argument boundaries. Positional
secrets and unrecognized credential names cannot be detected automatically.
.PP
Journal lines carried for units in trouble are redacted separately, by
\fB[units] redact_logs\fR (true by default), on the unit's own machine before
they reach a snapshot, so recordings, \fB\-\-remote\fR, notifications and the
\fBu\fR popup all get them redacted. Only a credential's value is hidden
\(em after a credential-looking \fIkey\fB=\fR, \fIkey\fB:\fR or quoted key, an
\fBAuthorization:\fR scheme, \fBBearer\fR, or in a URL's user and password
\(em and the rest of the line is kept, since it says why the unit failed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For \fB\-\-watch\fR, this means the condition was never met before
\fB\-\-watch\-timeout\fR elapsed.
.TP
.B 1
A \fB\-\-watch\fR condition matched; \fB\-\-doctor\fR found something the
config asks for that cannot work here (including a \fB[[notify]]\fR or
\fB[[silence]]\fR entry, which \fB\-\-doctor\fR reports rather than refusing);
or the run failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line; a \fB[[notify]]\fR target that cannot deliver; a
\fB[[silence]]\fR window that cannot open; or a
\fB\-\-watch\fR that cannot be run \(em a malformed query or rule, or a
\fBdisk\fR or \fBfillin\fR rule naming a mount crabmon does not measure, or a
\fBunit\fR or \fBkernel\fR rule whose source cannot be read here.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/crabmon/config.toml
Configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/crabmon/actions.log
The action log of signals, renices, affinity and IO-priority changes, and unit
restarts and \fBreset\-failed\fRs, unless
\fB[audit] path\fR says otherwise; moved to \fIactions.log.1\fR at
\fB[audit] max_bytes\fR.
